foreign  howdy y'all my name is dioc and I'm a  customer engineer at Google Cloud hello  everyone my name is Daniel and I'm a  custom engineer at Google Cloud welcome  back to the technical guides for  startups build series  I'm very excited to help you grow your  business successfully and sustainably on  Google Cloud  in our last video we covered excellent  content on analytics and artificial  intelligence jeevanin Hussein introduced  vertex Ai and what it can offer you as  you leverage data to provide for better  customer experiences  the next step in this journey is aimed  at solving the age-old question of how  to modernize an application  to achieve this let's talk about API  management  in this session we'll answer the  following questions  what's an API why do we need apis  managing your apis on Google Cloud API  management with apogee and choosing a  pricing model  we'll show you a demo on how to create  and manage an API with apogee and at the  end we'll look into how apigee supports  a customer with millions of API calls  per second  so let's Dive In  so what is an API API stands for  application programming interface apis  Define the rules of communication  between software applications you can  think of apis as the intermediaries that  standardize access to data between  applications  for example if you have a service  exposing information to the Internet you  may want to control the types of  interactions with data how frequently  resources can be accessed and how to  expose potentially sensitive information  so why do we need API management  as your services grow so will your  consumer base and you will want to  ensure your apis stay consistent and  reliable that your services are  discoverable and well documented and  that services are protected against  threats  now that we agree on one of the API is  why do we need one  first let's look at life without apis  in this example you can see a retailer  and the flow that one of their customers  would go through to use a Retailer's  online services  the customer will initiate a connection  to one of three clients web app mobile  app  or their in-store point of sale system  these clients will in turn consume the  services running in the Retailer's back  end through common user flows such as  adding medicine to their online cart  through their phone making a payment in  store or checking the balance of their  Rewards program through the web app  there are three major issues at play  here inefficient developer workflow  inconsistent customer experience and  difficult infrastructure maintenance  to see how this setup makes developers  work less productively let's observe how  for each client web app mobile and point  of sale the half templement calls to  back-end endpoints separately  even if there's code sharing or some  sort of best practices across the  company  there will be three separate systems  accessing the same resources  on issue number two when a customer acts  as a system through the web app and then  through the mobile app they'll be using  the different implementations that I  just mentioned  and could therefore not have access to  the same information through all the  three clients  imagine this you go to the store trying  to redeem rewards and you find out you  can only do so through your mobile app  where then you're looking for shipping  tracking info on an order that you  placed and you can only find that on the  web app but not the mobile app  finally the third issue is difficulty  maintaining the infrastructure this  arises from the need of the IT team to  support three separate teams each  consuming the endpoints with different  requirements and issues  consider this slight Improvement on the  setup we were looking at before  now each separate client makes requests  to the same Gateway where the apis  establish a structure on what needs to  be passed as input Inwood gets passed as  output  this decoupling of the front and back  end solves the previous issues and  allows for faster changes to both of  these systems  let's take our example even further  let's say you have many of such apis  imagine hundreds of different apis the  issue that arises now can't be fixed  with just having an API Gateway the  challenge now is how to manage all of  these apis at scale having to apply  policies such as rate limiting quotas  and authentication getting rich  visibility through monitoring and  Reporting on API consumption and  productizing apis so that they're no  longer just an interface to data or some  sort of connective tissue between text  systems but instead become a revenue  stream for your business  the solution for these challenges  is moving away from API gateways towards  API management  apogee is a Google Cloud's native API  management solution  although API management encompasses more  than a simpler API Gateway it may not be  the best option for your use case  let's go through this decision tree to  determine when to use one versus the  other  start with where you want to run your  services  if deploying them on Google Cloud you  first determine if you're implementing  them as serverless functions such as  Cloud functions or Cloud run if so then  the recommended route is Google Cloud's  API Gateway  alternatively if you need to manage this  service and run the co-located Gateway  or if you need private networking for  your endpoints the choice is clear Cloud  endpoints  lastly if you need lifecycle API  functionality such as building managing  or securing apis then the choice is  apogee  back to where you can run your services  if you decide to do so in a hybrid  architecture apogee through its hybrid  functionality is the right path  now let's dive in into apogee a bit more  let's explore some of our biggest  features apigee allows you to operate  your apis with enhanced scaled security  and automation you can build apis to  unlock and standardize data from any app  system or service you can secure every  API transaction without overhead using  robust policies and advanced controls  you can monitor and analyze your API  traffic to gain Brazilian performance  and you can create API products to  generate new revenue streams and vibrant  communities  a policy is like a module that  implements a specific function because  they are designed to let you easily add  common types of management capabilities  to an API they can save you time  implementing and maintaining a number of  functionalities for security traffic  management transformation and mediation  with traffic management policies you can  use apogee to minimize latency for apps  while maintaining the health of back-end  services  the spike arrest policy for example  protects against traffic searches this  policy limits the number of requests  processed by an API proxy and sent to a  back end and this protects against  performance lacks and downtime  with transformation policies apigee  makes manipulating messages flowing  through your API proxy simple for  example you can use the XML to Json or  XSL transform policies to make it easier  to transform messages between formats or  you can use the assign message policy to  create messages from variable values and  use the extract variable policy to  assign variables from message content  you're not limited to the set of policy  types provided by apogee you can write  custom scripts using JavaScript for  example and this allows you to innovate  on basic management capabilities  thanks Daniel  API security is a growing concern as API  traffic rose with ever increasing attack  vectors it's critical for the operations  team to protect the data flowing through  every API transaction with fine grain  control  apogee enables you to enforce consistent  security best practices and governance  policies detect bot attacks and analyze  potential threats with AI powered  monitoring to protect your data against  the most common security threats  apogee is also seamlessly integrated  with Google Cloud security products for  example it's easy to combine apogee with  Cloud armor and recaptcha for a full web  application and API protection solution  the rule of monitoring here is to ensure  apis are always available secure and  Performing as it's expected  API monitoring provides near real-time  insights into API traffic and  performance to help you one quickly  diagnose and solve issues as they arise  by letting you create alerts on changes  in API traffic so you can take  appropriate action before customers are  affected two increase API availability  and reduce the mean time to diagnosis  mttd through quick investigations  three  leverage fault codes to speed up  diagnosis and four isolate problem areas  quickly to diagnose errors performance  and latency issues and their sources APD  offers developer portal solutions that  enable app developers to learn about  their data and service offerings learn  how to use your apis about review and  documentation including example use  cases tutorials and reference  documentation register a self-service  process in order to build apps that use  your apis  you will learn more about the developer  portal later in this video  that's quite a bit of info on apogee if  this sounds like what you're looking for  then the next step is to determine how  to consume it  there are two pricing models  a subscription that has tiers  and you can choose which tier is best  for use case  in a newly added pay as you go model  this new model has no upfront costs and  requires no commitment  the charges will be based solely on your  usage of three things  apogee Gateway nodes per minute which  are units of an environment that  processes the API traffic  two total number of API requests  processed by apogee analytics whether  the requests are successful or not and  three General Google Cloud networking  costs which are not specific to apigee  next up we'll show you a demo on how to  create and manage apis with apogee in  this 